  "intro": {
    "en": "In April a small bird lands on a telephone wire, and summer has begun. Swallows, martins and swifts spend their winters in Africa and come back here to breed. They catch insects in the air, so you mostly see them in flight.\n\nThat makes them tricky for beginners: they are fast, and they don't sit still. But each one has a clear mark. A forked tail with long streamers, a white rump, a brown band across the chest. And one of the four is not a swallow at all, despite how it looks.\n\nAt the end there's a quiz of eight questions, with birds from the last two lessons mixed in."
  },
